    "text": "To implement the function `pmod` correctly, we need to compute \\(2^n^n \\mod p`\\). This operation can be efficiently done using modular arithmetic. Here's the complete function implementation:\n):\n\n```python\ndef modp(n: int, p: int):\n    \"\"\"Return 2^n modulo p (be aware of numerics).\n    >>> modp(3, 5)\n    3\n    >>> modp(1101, 101)\n    2\n    >>> modp(0, 101)\n    1\n    >>> modp(3, 11)\n    8\n    >>> modp(100, 101)\n    1\n    \"\"\"\n    return (2 ** n) % p\n```\n\nThis function uses the `**` operator (%) to compute the power and the modulo `` operator to get the result modulo \\(p\\). The `%` operator is used to find the remainder when the",
